ting**: Jha et al. (arXiv 2502.18049, 2025)\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n- All experiments on controlled injection (not natural contamination)\n- Effect sizes: ~0.055 PPL at ρ=0.4 (0.6B model), ~0.020 PPL at 3B model\n- Downstream metrics (MMLU, HellaSwag) show no statistically distinguishable improvement\n- The paper's primary contribution is a negative finding (α_eff is harmful/inert) plus a simple practical recommendation (ESS-only)\n\n## Method Details\n- Weighting: wi = (1-qi)^b, applied as direct loss reweighting (wi/w̄ normalized)\n- b* solved via bisection on [0, b_max] per micro-batch\n- Lightweight detector: TF-IDF + LR (AUC=0.939), trained on 10K samples in <2 min\n- Strong detector: ModernBERT-base (AUC=0.986)\n- The dual-constrained formulation uses α_eff (effective real fraction) ≥ τ and ESS ≥ n_min\n- ESS-only just uses ESS ≥ n_min, choosing max b\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Novelty**: How novel are the adaptive b* formulations? Is the negative finding (α_eff harmful) genuinely surprising or predictable? Does the ESS-only recommendation constitute sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are important related works missing? Is the positioning against prior work accurate and fair?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact**: Given the small effect sizes and lack of downstream impact, how significant is this work? Would practitioners benefit from this? Is the negative finding valuable to the community?\n\n4. **Framing and Claims**: Are the claims appropriately calibrated to the evidence? Is the paper honest about limitations?\n\n5. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Are arguments clearly presented?\n\nProvide your assessment in this format:\n\n### R2 Assessment: Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths**\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n**Weaknesses**\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n**Questions for Authors**\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n**Missing Related Work**\n- ...\n\n**Prel