{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0175pe824bdxUAVhWDbKyXXA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n6\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n7\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n14\t\\usepackage{algpseudocode}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\bstar}{b^{*}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\aeff}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\nmin}{n_{\\min}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\bESS}{b_{\\mathrm{ESS}}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\btau}{b_{\\tau}}\n21\t\n22\t\\title{Dual-Constrained Adaptive Reweighting for\\\\Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining}\n23\t\n24\t\\author{Anonymous}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{abstract}\n30\tWeb corpora used for language model pretraining are increasingly contaminated by machine-generated text, with estimated synthetic proportions of 20--60\\% and unknown provenance.\n31\tPrior detect-and-reweight methods assign sample weights $w=(1-q)^b$ based on detector scores $q$, but require per-model validation-based grid search over the exponent $b$.\n32\tWe propose a \\emph{dual-constrained adaptive} $\\bstar$ that eliminates this grid search by deriving $b$ per-batch from the empirical $q$-score distribution, subject to two constraints: an effective real-data fraction lower bound ($\\aeff(b) \\geq \\tau$) ensuring sufficient decontamination, and an effective sample size ceiling ($\\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq \\nmin$) preventing over-downweighting.\n33\tThe solution $\\bstar = \\inf\\{b \\geq 0 : \\aeff(b) \\geq \\tau\\}$ subject to $\\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq \\nmin$ embodies a \\emph{minimum intervention principle}: apply only as much downweighting as needed to meet the quality threshold.\n34\tIn controlled experiments on Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B (200M tokens, 40--60\\% contamination), adaptive $\\bstar$ matches grid-search-optimal performance within seed variance at zero validation cost.\n35\tAn ESS-only ablation confirms that the $\\aeff$ quality floor is load-bearing: removing it degrades performance to near-uniform levels.\n36\tThe method is insensitive to $\\tau$ (PPL range 0.023 across $\\tau \\in [0.5, 0.95]$), works with lightweight detectors (TF-IDF+LR, AUC=0.939), and scales favorably with contamination severity and model size (4$\\times$ effect amplification at 3B parameters).\n37\t\\end{abstract}\n38\t\n39\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t\\label{sec:intro}\n42\t\n43\tThe rapid proliferation of large language models has created a feedback loop in which model-generated text increasingly contaminates web-crawled pretraining corpora \\cite{shumailov2024model}.\n44\tEstimates place the synthetic fraction of common crawl data at 20--60\\%, with the generating model and contamination proportion typically unknown at training time.\n45\tEven moderate contamination degrades model quality: Strong Model Collapse theory demonstrates that a small fraction of synthetic data suffices to distort scaling laws.\n46\t\n47\tA natural mitigation is \\emph{detect-and-reweight}: train a contamination detector to produce per-sample scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$, then downweight likely-synthetic samples via $w_i = (1 - q_i)^b$.\n48\tDrayson et al.\\ establish this framework, but their exponent $b$ is a hyperparameter requiring per-model validation-based grid search---GPT-2 and SmolLM $\\leq$360M use $b{=}10$, while SmolLM-1.7B uses $b{=}1$---making the approach costly and non-transferable.\n49\tGolden Ratio weighting provides a theoretically motivated fixed weight but assumes known contamination proportions.\n50\t\n51\tWe propose \\textbf{dual-constrained adaptive $\\bstar$}, which derives $b$ per-batch from two principled constraints:\n52\t\\begin{enumerate}\n53\t    \\item \\textbf{Quality floor} ($\\aeff(b) \\geq \\tau$): the weighted effective real-data fraction must exceed a convergence-motivated threshold $\\tau$, ensuring ``at least enough decontamination.''\n54\t    \\item \\textbf{Stability ceiling} ($\\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq \\nmin$): the Kish effective sample size must remain above a minimum, preventing ``too much decontamination.''\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\tSince $\\aeff(b)$ is monotonically increasing and $\\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ monotonically decreasing in $b$, the two constraints carve out a feasible interval $[\\btau, \\bESS]$, and the solution $\\bstar = \\btau$ (when feasible) is the \\emph{minimum intervention} point.\n58\tIn approximately 40\\% of reweighted batches, $\\aeff$ is the binding constraint, choosing $\\bstar = 1.67$ versus $\\bESS = 4.15$ under ESS-only selection---a 2.5$\\times$ reduction in intervention intensity.\n59\t\n60\tOur contributions:\n61\t\\begin{itemize}\n62\t    \\item A principled, per-batch adaptive method for selecting the reweighting exponent $b$ that requires no validation set, no known contamination proportion, and no per-model tuning.\n63\t    \\item Empirical demonstration that adaptive $\\bstar$ matches grid-search-optimal $b$ within seed variance across three seeds, eliminating the cost of $6N$-to-$1$ grid search runs.\n64\t    \\item Evidence that the $\\aeff$ quality floor is a load-bearing component: an ESS-only ablation (max-intervention) degrades PPL to near-uniform levels, while a $\\tau$ sweep shows the method is insensitive to the specific threshold value.\n65\t    \\item Scaling analysis: the effect amplifies 2.5$\\times$ from $\\rho{=}0.4$ to $\\rho{=}0.6$ contamination and 4$\\times$ from 0.6B to 3B parameters, with consistent downstream task improvements.\n66\t\\end{itemize}\n67\t\n68\t\n69\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n70\t\\section{Related Work}\n71\t\\label{sec:related}\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Synthetic Data Contamination.}\n74\tStrong Model Collapse establishes that even small fractions of synthetic data in pretraining corpora degrade scaling behavior.\n75\tThe problem is compounded by the difficulty of identifying the generating model and contamination proportion in web-scale data.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Detect-and-Reweight.}\n78\tDrayson et al.\\ propose $w_i = (1 - q_i)^b$ with detector scores $q_i$ and use SIR resampling.\n79\tTheir $b$ requires per-model validation: different model scales need different $b$ values.\n80\tWe replace this empirical search with a principled per-batch derivation via dual constraints.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Golden Ratio Weighting.}\n83\tThe Golden Ratio approach assigns $w^* = 1/\\varphi \\approx 0.618$ for synthetic samples, providing a theoretically motivated weight under known proportions.\n84\tWe use it as an oracle baseline, showing that per-batch adaptive $\\bstar$ outperforms even proportion-aware fixed weighting.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Data Quality Optimization.}\n87\tDomain-level methods like DoReMi and DSIR optimize mixture weights at the corpus level, while SoftDedup applies instance-level deduplication-based reweighting.\n88\tDynamic loss-based reweighting uses per-sample loss signals without detector scores.\n89\tOur work operates at the instance level using detector scores, complementing these approaches.\n90\t\n91\t\n92\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n93\t\\section{Method}\n94\t\\label{sec:method}\n95\t\n96\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n97\t\n98\tConsider pretraining on a corpus $\\mathcal{D} = \\{(x_i, q_i)\\}_{i=1}^{N}$, where $x_i$ is a text sample and $q_i \\in [0,1]$ is a contamination score from a pre-trained detector ($q_i = 1$: certainly synthetic).\n99\tThe contamination proportion $\\rho = |\\{i : x_i \\text{ is synthetic}\\}|/N$ is \\emph{unknown}.\n100\tFollowing Drayson et al., we assign sample weights:\n101\t\\begin{equation}\n102\t    w_i = (1 - q_i)^b, \\quad b \\geq 0.\n103\t    \\label{eq:weight}\n104\t\\end{equation}\n105\tOur goal is to select $b$ adaptively per micro-batch without a validation set.\n106\t\n107\t\\subsection{Dual Constraints}\n108\t\n109\tFor a micro-batch $B = \\{(x_j, q_j)\\}_{j=1}^{|B|}$ with weights $w_j = (1-q_j)^b$, we define:\n110\t\n111\t\\paragraph{Effective real-data fraction.}\n112\t\\begin{equation}\n113\t    \\aeff(b) = \\frac{\\sum_{j \\in B} w_j \\cdot \\mathbf{1}[q_j \\leq 0.5]}{\\sum_{j \\in B} w_j},\n114\t    \\label{eq:alpha_eff}\n115\t\\end{equation}\n116\twhich measures the weighted proportion of likely-real samples.\n117\t$\\aeff(b)$ is monotonically non-decreasing in $b$: increasing $b$ downweights high-$q$ samples more, raising the real fraction.\n118\t\n119\t\\paragraph{Effective sample size (Kish's ESS).}\n120\t\\begin{equation}\n121\t    \\mathrm{ESS}(b) = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{j \\in B} w_j\\right)^2}{\\sum_{j \\in B} w_j^2},\n122\t    \\label{eq:ess}\n123\t\\end{equation}\n124\twhich estimates the number of effectively independent samples contributing to the gradient.\n125\t$\\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ is monotonically non-increasing in $b$: more aggressive downweighting reduces effective sample diversity.\n126\t\n127\t\\subsection{Adaptive $\\bstar$ Selection}\n128\t\\label{sec:bstar}\n129\t\n130\tWe solve:\n131\t\\begin{equation}\n132\t    \\bstar = \\inf\\{b \\geq 0 : \\aeff(b) \\geq \\tau\\} \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq \\nmin.\n133\t    \\label{eq:bstar}\n134\t\\end{equation}\n135\t\n136\t\\textbf{Minimum intervention principle:} $\\bstar$ is the smallest $b$ that achieves quality threshold $\\tau$.\n137\tThe ESS constraint provides a safety ceiling: if $\\btau > \\bESS$, we fall back to $\\bstar = \\bESS$ to preserve gradient stability.\n138\t\n139\t\\paragraph{Constraint interaction.}\n140\tSince $\\aeff$ gives a lower bound $\\btau$ and ESS gives an upper bound $\\bESS$:\n141\t\\begin{itemize}\n142\t    \\item If $\\aeff(0) \\geq \\tau$ (batch is already clean): $\\bstar = 0$ (no reweighting).\n143\t    \\item If $\\btau \\leq \\bESS$ ($\\aeff$ binding): $\\bstar = \\btau$ (minimum intervention).\n144\t    \\item If $\\btau > \\bESS$ (ESS binding): $\\bstar = \\bESS$ (stability-limited).\n145\t\\end{itemize}\n146\t\n147\t\\paragraph{Implementation.}\n148\t$\\bstar$ is found via bisection search over $b \\in [0, b_{\\max}]$ per micro-batch, adding negligible overhead ($<$1\\% wall time).\n149\tThe weighted training loss is:\n150\t\\begin{equation}\n151\t    \\mathcal{L}_B = \\frac{\\sum_{j \\in B} w_j \\cdot \\ell(x_j; \\theta)}{\\sum_{j \\in B} w_j},\n152\t\\end{equation}\n153\twhere $\\ell(x_j; \\theta)$ is the per-sample language modeling loss.\n154\t\n155\t\\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}\n156\t$\\tau$ and $\\nmin$ are dataset-agnostic defaults set once: $\\tau = 0.8$, $\\nmin = 2$.\n157\tUnlike Drayson et al.'s $b$, which must be re-tuned per model and dataset, $\\tau$ and $\\nmin$ are empirically insensitive (\\S\\ref{sec:tau_sweep}, \\S\\ref{sec:nmin_ablation}).\n158\t\n159\t\n160\t\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n161\t\\caption{Per-batch adaptive $\\bstar$ selection}\n162\t\\label{alg:bstar}\n163\t\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n164\t\\Require Micro-batch scores $\\{q_j\\}_{j=1}^{|B|}$, threshold $\\tau$, minimum ESS $\\nmin$\n165\t\\If{$\\aeff(0) \\geq \\tau$} \\Comment{Batch already clean}\n166\t    \\State \\Return $\\bstar = 0$\n167\t\\EndIf\n168\t\\State $\\btau \\gets$ bisect $\\{b : \\aeff(b) = \\tau\\}$ over $[0, b_{\\max}]$\n169\t\\State $\\bESS \\gets$ bisect $\\{b : \\mathrm{ESS}(b) = \\nmin\\}$ over $[0, b_{\\max}]$\n170\t\\State $\\bstar \\gets \\min(\\btau, \\bESS)$\n171\t\\State Compute weights $w_j = (1 - q_j)^{\\bstar}$\n172\t\\State \\Return $\\bstar$, $\\{w_j\\}$\n173\t\\end{algorithmic}\n174\t\\end{algorithm}\n175\t\n176\t\n177\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n178\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n179\t\\label{sec:setup}\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n182\tQwen3-0.6B (primary) and Llama-3.2-3B (scaling validation), both trained from their pretrained checkpoints on 200M tokens.\n183\t\n184\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n185\tWe construct controlled contamination scenarios from FineWeb.\n186\tReal data is sampled directly; synthetic data is generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-generator) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-generator) via prompted continuation.\n187\tDefault contamination $\\rho = 0.4$ (40\\% synthetic); we also test $\\rho = 0.6$.\n188\t\n189\t\\paragraph{Detector.}\n190\tTF-IDF + Logistic Regression (AUC = 0.939, calibrated ECE = 0.048) serves as the primary detector.\n191\tModernBERT-150M (AUC = 0.986, ECE = 0.022) provides a detector quality ablation.\n192\t\n193\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n194\t\\begin{itemize}\n195\t    \\item \\textbf{Uniform}: standard training with equal weights ($b=0$).\n196\t    \\item \\textbf{Grid-$b$}: Drayson-style grid search over $b \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, \\ldots, 5.0\\}$ with holdout selection.\n197\t    \\item \\textbf{ESS-only}: $\\max\\{b : \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq \\nmin\\}$---maximum intervention.\n198\t    \\item \\textbf{Golden Ratio oracle}: $w = 1/\\varphi \\approx 0.618$ for synthetic ($q > 0.5$), $w = 1.0$ for real, using known $\\rho$.\n199\t    \\item \\textbf{Detect-and-remove}: hard threshold at $q = 0.5$, discard synthetic samples.\n200\t    \\item \\textbf{Random reweighting}: i.i.d.\\ uniform $[0,1]$ weights (no detector signal)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Po6GWztU94UYxmsY1CE6NF","session_id":"4e76514d-f28c-4641-af3a-08051cc17412","uuid":"bd408248-0055-4b92-ade4-27e9bb12b58f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:14:55.578Z"}
