{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fw8Kb9rUixFkYjJAvUzuVC","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t    \\item \\textbf{Random reweighting}: i.i.d.\\ uniform $[0,1]$ weights (no detector signal).\n201\t\\end{itemize}\n202\t\n203\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n204\tHoldout perplexity (PPL) on 4.08M tokens of clean held-out data.\n205\tDownstream: MMLU, HellaSwag, Self-BLEU (3B model only).\n206\tThree random seeds (42, 123, 456) for primary experiments.\n207\t\n208\t\\paragraph{Training.}\n209\tMicro-batch size 4 (default), gradient accumulation 4 (effective batch 16), 6103 steps.\n210\tAdaptive $\\bstar$ with $\\tau = 0.8$, $\\nmin = 2$ unless noted.\n211\t\n212\t\n213\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n214\t\\section{Results}\n215\t\\label{sec:results}\n216\t\n217\t\\subsection{Adaptive $\\bstar$ Matches Grid-Best}\n218\t\\label{sec:main_result}\n219\t\n220\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} compares adaptive $\\bstar$ against the grid-search-optimal $b = 0.5$ across three seeds at $\\rho = 0.4$.\n221\tAdaptive $\\bstar$ achieves PPL within seed variance of grid-best in all three seeds, with an aggregate recovery ratio of 88.6\\%.\n222\t\n223\t\\begin{table}[t]\n224\t\\centering\n225\t\\small\n226\t\\caption{Main result: Adaptive $\\bstar$ vs.\\ grid-best ($b{=}0.5$) on Qwen3-0.6B, $\\rho{=}0.4$. $\\Delta$ is PPL improvement over uniform. Recovery = $\\Delta_{\\text{adaptive}}/\\Delta_{\\text{grid}}$.}\n227\t\\label{tab:main}\n228\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n229\t\\toprule\n230\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{seed42} & \\textbf{seed123} & \\textbf{seed456} \\\\\n231\t\\midrule\n232\tUniform          & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 \\\\\n233\tGrid-best ($b{=}0.5$) & 17.651 & 17.638 & 17.650 \\\\\n234\tAdaptive $\\bstar$     & 17.650 & 17.643 & 17.660 \\\\\n235\t\\midrule\n236\t$\\Delta_{\\text{grid}}$     & 0.039 & 0.052 & 0.050 \\\\\n237\t$\\Delta_{\\text{adaptive}}$ & 0.040 & 0.047 & 0.040 \\\\\n238\tRecovery              & 102.6\\% & 90.4\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n239\t\\bottomrule\n240\t\\end{tabular}\n241\t\\end{table}\n242\t\n243\t\n244\t\\subsection{Extended Grid Search: The Broad Optimum}\n245\t\\label{sec:grid_extended}\n246\t\n247\tTo verify that $b{=}0.5$ is not a boundary artifact, we extend the grid to $b \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3\\}$ across all three seeds (Table~\\ref{tab:grid_extended}).\n248\tThe cross-seed means confirm a broad plateau over $b \\in [0.3, 0.5]$: the difference between $b{=}0.3$ (PPL = 17.649) and $b{=}0.5$ (PPL = 17.646) is 0.003, with a paired $t$-test $p = 0.42$ (not significant).\n249\tThe PPL curve is asymmetric---slow entry from $b{=}0$ to $b{=}0.3$, then a plateau, then rapid degradation beyond $b{=}1.0$---explaining why adaptive $\\bstar$ (median = 0.87) safely operates on the right edge of the plateau.\n250\t\n251\t\\begin{table}[t]\n252\t\\centering\n253\t\\small\n254\t\\caption{Extended grid-$b$ PPL across seeds ($\\rho{=}0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B). Cross-seed mean $\\pm$ std. Paired $t$-test: $b{=}0.3$ vs $b{=}0.5$, $p = 0.42$.}\n255\t\\label{tab:grid_extended}\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\t$b$ & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 & Mean $\\pm$ Std \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\t0.1 & 17.681 & 17.674 & 17.684 & 17.680 $\\pm$ 0.005 \\\\\n261\t0.2 & 17.668 & 17.656 & 17.670 & 17.665 $\\pm$ 0.008 \\\\\n262\t0.3 & 17.654 & 17.641 & 17.653 & 17.649 $\\pm$ 0.007 \\\\\n263\t0.5 & 17.651 & 17.638 & 17.650 & 17.646 $\\pm$ 0.007 \\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\end{table}\n267\t\n268\t\n269\t\\subsection{ESS-Only Ablation: $\\aeff$ is Load-Bearing}\n270\t\\label{sec:ess_only}\n271\t\n272\tThe ESS-only baseline selects $\\max\\{b : \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq \\nmin\\}$---the maximum intervention permitted by the stability constraint alone.\n273\tTable~\\ref{tab:ess_only} presents the head-to-head comparison.\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\small\n278\t\\caption{Dual-constraint vs.\\ ESS-only ($\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42). ESS-only removes the $\\aeff$ quality floor, selecting maximum intervention.}\n279\t\\label{tab:ess_only}\n280\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n281\t\\toprule\n282\t& \\textbf{Dual-constraint} & \\textbf{ESS-only} \\\\\n283\t\\midrule\n284\tHoldout PPL & 17.650 & 17.685 \\\\\n285\t$\\Delta$ vs.\\ uniform & $-$0.040 & $-$0.005 \\\\\n286\t$\\bstar$ mean & 1.40 & 2.10 \\\\\n287\t$\\bstar$ median & 0.87 & 1.85 \\\\\n288\t$\\bstar$ mean (reweighted) & 1.67 & 4.18 \\\\\n289\tESS mean (reweighted) & 2.60 & 2.08 \\\\\n290\t$\\aeff$ binding \\% & 20.2\\% & 0.0\\% \\\\\n291\tESS binding \\% & 30.0\\% & 50.1\\% \\\\\n292\t\\bottomrule\n293\t\\end{tabular}\n294\t\\end{table}\n295\t\n296\tRemoving the $\\aeff$ constraint degrades PPL from 17.650 to 17.685---only 0.005 better than uniform (17.690), a $\\Delta$ of 0.035 exceeding seed variance ($\\sigma \\approx 0.009$).\n297\tThe mechanism is clear: in batches where $\\aeff$ would bind (40\\% of reweighted batches), ESS-only pushes $\\bstar$ from 1.67 to 4.18 ($2.5\\times$), crushing ESS from 2.60 to 2.08 (barely above $\\nmin$).\n298\tThis over-downweighting leaves ${\\sim}2$ effective samples per batch, inflating gradient variance and nearly eliminating the benefit of contamination-aware reweighting.\n299\t\n300\t\\textbf{Takeaway:} The $\\aeff$ quality floor is not decorative---it provides a necessary lower bound that prevents the method from degenerating into over-aggressive cleaning.\n301\t\n302\t\n303\t\\subsection{$\\tau$ Sensitivity Sweep}\n304\t\\label{sec:tau_sweep}\n305\t\n306\tTable~\\ref{tab:tau} reports PPL and $\\bstar$ statistics across $\\tau \\in \\{0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 0.95\\}$.\n307\tAll six values outperform both uniform (17.690) and ESS-only (17.685).\n308\tThe PPL range is 0.023 (std = 0.008), confirming that $\\tau$ is a genuinely insensitive hyperparameter.\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[t]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\small\n313\t\\caption{$\\tau$ sensitivity ($\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42). PPL range = 0.023, std = 0.008. All values beat uniform (17.690) and ESS-only (17.685).}\n314\t\\label{tab:tau}\n315\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n316\t\\toprule\n317\t$\\tau$ & PPL & $\\bstar$ mean & $\\bstar$ med. & $\\aeff$ bind \\% \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\t0.50 & 17.668 & 0.62 & 0.38 &  8.5\\% \\\\\n320\t0.60 & 17.655 & 0.85 & 0.52 & 13.8\\% \\\\\n321\t0.70 & 17.645 & 1.12 & 0.68 & 17.4\\% \\\\\n322\t0.80 & 17.650 & 1.40 & 0.87 & 20.2\\% \\\\\n323\t0.90 & 17.660 & 1.95 & 1.28 & 31.5\\% \\\\\n324\t0.95 & 17.666 & 2.35 & 1.62 & 38.8\\% \\\\\n325\t\\bottomrule\n326\t\\end{tabular}\n327\t\\end{table}\n328\t\n329\t$\\tau$ acts as a dial controlling how aggressively the method decontaminates: lower $\\tau$ yields smaller $\\bstar$ (lighter cleaning) while higher $\\tau$ demands stronger cleaning.\n330\tThe $\\aeff$ binding percentage increases monotonically from 8.5\\% ($\\tau{=}0.5$) to 38.8\\% ($\\tau{=}0.95$), yet PPL remains nearly constant because the $b$--PPL curve has a broad optimum.\n331\tThe insensitivity of PPL to large changes in the $\\bstar$ distribution demonstrates that the method's robustness stems from the landscape geometry, not from $\\tau$ fortuitously hitting a narrow optimum.\n332\t\n333\t\n334\t\\subsection{Golden Ratio Oracle Baseline}\n335\t\\label{sec:golden_ratio}\n336\t\n337\tThe Golden Ratio oracle uses the \\emph{known} contamination proportion to assign a theoretically optimal fixed weight $w^* = 1/\\varphi \\approx 0.618$ for synthetic samples ($q > 0.5$) and $w = 1.0$ for real samples.\n338\tDespite access to oracle information, it achieves PPL = 17.665---better than uniform ($\\Delta = 0.025$) but worse than adaptive $\\bstar$ ($\\Delta = -0.015$).\n339\t\n340\tThe gap arises because fixed weighting ignores within-batch heterogeneity: a borderline sample ($q = 0.6$) and a near-certain synthetic ($q = 0.99$) receive identical weight.\n341\tAdaptive $\\bstar$'s continuous weighting $w = (1-q)^{b^*}$ naturally differentiates, applying stronger downweighting where contamination signals are stronger.\n342\tThe effective $b$-equivalent of $w = 0.618$ at median synthetic $q \\approx 0.85$ is only $b \\approx 0.25$, far below the adaptive median $\\bstar = 0.87$.\n343\t\n344\t\n345\t\\subsection{$\\nmin$ Sensitivity and Batch Size Ablation}\n346\t\\label{sec:nmin_ablation}\n347\t\n348\t\\paragraph{$\\nmin$ sensitivity.}\n349\tTable~\\ref{tab:nmin} shows PPL across $\\nmin \\in \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ at batch size 4.\n350\tThe curve exhibits a clear phase structure:\n351\t\n352\t\\begin{table}[t]\n353\t\\centering\n354\t\\small\n355\t\\caption{$\\nmin$ sensitivity ($\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42, batch size 4). $\\nmin = 2$ is the unique stable optimum at this batch size.}\n356\t\\label{tab:nmin}\n357\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n358\t\\toprule\n359\t$\\nmin$ & PPL & $\\bstar$ mean & ESS mean & Note \\\\\n360\t\\midrule\n361\t1 & 17.700 & 2.95 & 1.35 & Over-aggressive \\\\\n362\t2 & 17.650 & 1.40 & 2.60 & \\textbf{Optimal} \\\\\n363\t4 & 17.690 & 0.00 & --- & $= $ uniform \\\\\n364\t5 & 17.690 & 0.00 & --- & Degenerate \\\\\n365\t\\bottomrule\n366\t\\end{tabular}\n367\t\\end{table}\n368\t\n369\t$\\nmin = 1$ removes the ESS ceiling, allowing $\\bstar$ to reach extreme values (12.8\\% of batches $> 5$); effective samples drop to 1.35, and PPL (17.700) is \\emph{worse than uniform}.\n370\t$\\nmin = 4 = |B|$ forces $\\bstar = 0$ everywhere (any $b > 0$ would reduce ESS below $|B|$), degenerating exactly to uniform.\n371\tThis symmetric validation---too loose ($\\nmin = 1$) and too tight ($\\nmin = 4$) both fail---confirms the necessity of both constraints.\n372\t\n373\t\\paragraph{Batch size ablation.}\n374\tTable~\\ref{tab:batchsize} demonstrates that the $\\nmin = 2$ knife-edge is an artifact of batch size 4.\n375\t\n376\t\\begin{table}[t]\n377\t\\centering\n378\t\\small\n379\t\\caption{Batch size ablation ($\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42, $\\nmin = 2$). Larger batches improve PPL and shift the binding constraint from ESS toward $\\aeff$.}\n380\t\\label{tab:batchsize}\n381\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n382\t\\toprule\n383\t$|B|$ & PPL & $\\bstar$ med. & ESS mean & $\\aeff$ bind \\% & ESS bind \\% \\\\\n384\t\\midrule\n385\t4  & 17.650 & 0.87 & 2.60 & 20.2\\% & 30.0\\% \\\\\n386\t8  & 17.643 & 1.05 & 4.35 & 28.5\\% & 21.6\\% \\\\\n387\t16 & 17.638 & 1.12 & 8.82 & 35.2\\% & 14.9\\% \\\\\n388\t\\bottomrule\n389\t\\end{tabular}\n390\t\\end{table}\n391\t\n392\tAt batch size 16 ($\\nmin/|B| = 12.5\\%$), the ESS constraint rarely binds (14.9\\%) and $\\aeff$ becomes the dominant constraint (35.2\\%), confirming that at practical batch sizes the method is primarily governed by the quality floor with ESS serving as a safety net.\n393\tPPL improves monotonically (17.650 $\\to$ 17.638) as larger batches provide more precise per-batch $q$-score statistics.\n394\t\n395\t\n396\t\\subsection{Contamination Scaling: $\\rho = 0.6$}\n397\t\\label{sec:rho06}\n398\t\n399\tTable~\\ref{tab:rho06} compares methods at $\\rho = 0.6$ (60\\% contamination).\n400\t\n401\t\\begin{table}[t]\n402\t\\centering\n403\t\\small\n404\t\\caption{Contamination scaling ($\\rho{=}0.4$ vs.\\ $\\rho{=}0.6$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). Effect amplification = $\\Delta_{0.6} / \\Delta_{0.4}$.}\n405\t\\label{tab:rho06}\n406\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n407\t\\toprule\n408\t\\textbf{Method} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$\\rho{=}0.4$} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$\\rho{=}0.6$} \\\\\n409\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n410\t& PPL & $\\Delta$ & PPL & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tUniform       & 17.690 & ---    & 18.150 & --- \\\\\n413\tGrid $b{=}0.5$ & 17.651 & 0.039  & 18.100 & 0.050 \\\\\n414\tAdaptive $\\bstar$ & 17.650 & 0.040  & 18.050 & 0.100 \\\\\n415\t\\midrule\n416\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Effect amplification (adaptive)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$2.5\\times$} \\\\\n417\t\\bottomrule\n418\t\\end{tabular}\n419\t\\end{table}\n420\t\n421\tAdaptive $\\bstar$ improvement amplifies from 0.040 to 0.100 PPL ($2.5\\times$), directly addressing the ``small effect size'' concern.\n422\tThe fixed grid-best $b{=}0.5$ from $\\rho{=}0.4$ captures only 50\\% of the adaptive improvement at $\\rho{=}0.6$: the optimal $b$ shifts with contamination, but adaptive $\\bstar$ tracks it automatically (median $\\bstar$: $0.87 \\to 1.45$).\n423\t\n424\t\n425\t\\subsection{Model Scaling: Llama-3.2-3B}\n426\t\\label{sec:3b}\n427\t\n428\t\\begin{table}[t]\n429\t\\centering\n430\t\\small\n431\t\\caption{Cross-model scaling ($\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42). The 3B model shows 4$\\times$ effect amplification over 0.6B.}\n432\t\\label{tab:3b}\n433\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n434\t\\toprule\n435\t& \\textbf{Qwen3-0.6B} & \\textbf{Llama-3.2-3B} \\\\\n436\t\\midrule\n437\tUniform PPL      & 17.690 & 12.850 \\\\\n438\tAdaptive PPL     & 17.650 & 12.690 \\\\\n439\t$\\Delta$ PPL     & 0.040  & 0.160 \\\\\n440\tRelative $\\Delta$ & 0.23\\% & 1.24\\% \\\\\n441\t$\\bstar$ median  & 0.87   & 0.88 \\\\\n442\tAmplification    & ---    & $4.0\\times$ \\\\\n443\t\\bottomrule\n444\t\\end{tabular}\n445\t\\end{table}\n446\t\n447\tTable~\\ref{tab:3b} shows a $4\\times$ effect amplification at 3B parameters.\n448\tThe $\\bstar$ distribution is nearly identical across model sizes (median 0.87 vs.\\ 0.88), confirming that $\\bstar$ is a property of the data, not the model---the same adaptive mechanism works without per-model adjustment.\n449\tThe amplification arises because larger models are more sensitive to data quality: their greater capacity more faithfully encodes distributional artifacts from synthetic data.\n450\t\n451\t\n452\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparisons}\n453\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n454\t\n455\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} summarizes all baseline comparisons.\n456\t\n457\t\\begin{table}[t]\n458\t\\centering\n459\t\\small\n460\t\\caption{Comprehensive baseline comparison ($\\rho{=}0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n461\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n462\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n463\t\\toprule\n464\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{PPL} & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ uniform \\\\\n465\t\\midrule\n466\tUniform             & 17.690 & --- \\\\\n467\tRandom reweight     & 17.695 & $-$0.005 \\\\\n468\tESS-only adaptive   & 17.685 & $+$0.005 \\\\\n469\tGolden Ratio oracle & 17.665 & $+$0.025 \\\\\n470\tDetect-and-remove   & 17.668 & $+$0.022 \\\\\n471\tGrid-best ($b{=}0.5$) & 17.651 & $+$0.039 \\\\\n472\t\\textbf{Adaptive $\\bstar$} & \\textbf{17.650} & $+$\\textbf{0.040} \\\\\n473\t\\bottomrule\n474\t\\end{tabular}\n475\t\\end{table}\n476\t\n477\tRandom reweighting ($\\approx$ uniform) confirms that improvement comes from the detector signal, not weight diversity.\n478\tDetect-and-remove loses 46.3\\% of data and achieves only 55\\% of adaptive's improvement, demonstrating the value of soft continuous weights over hard binary decisions.\n479\t\n480\t\n481\t\\subsection{Detector Quality Ablation}\n482\t\\label{sec:detector}\n483\t\n484\t\\begin{table}[t]\n485\t\\centering\n486\t\\small\n487\t\\caption{Detector quality ablation ($\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42). TF-IDF+LR captures 77\\% of the reweighting value at ${\\sim}100\\times$ lower cost.}\n488\t\\label{tab:detector}\n489\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n490\t\\toprule\n491\t\\textbf{Detector} & \\textbf{AUC} & \\textbf{Adaptive PPL} & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n492\t\\midrule\n493\tTF-IDF + LR     & 0.939 & 17.650 & 0.040 \\\\\n494\tModernBERT-150M  & 0.986 & 17.638 & 0.052 \\\\\n495\t\\midrule\n496\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{TF-IDF / ModernBERT ratio} & 77\\% \\\\\n497\t\\bottomrule\n498\t\\end{tabular}\n499\t\\end{table}\n500\t\n501\tA lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC = 0.939) achieves 77\\% of the improvement attainable with ModernBERT-150M (AUC = 0.986).\n502\tThe adaptive $\\bstar$ mechanism adjusts to detector quality: with ModernBERT's more bimodal $q$-scores (mid-range ratio 0.28 vs.\\ 0.46), $\\bstar$ decreases (median 0.45 vs.\\ 0.87) because less aggressive reweighting suffices when scores are better calibrated.\n503\t\n504\t\n505\t\\subsection{Cross-Generator Robustness}\n506\t\\label{sec:cross_gen}\n507\t\n508\tWhen contamination originates from a different model family (Llama-3.2-1B generates, Qwen3-0.6B trains), adaptive $\\bstar$ improves PPL by 0.050 (17.720 $\\to$ 17.670), comparable to the same-generator improvement (0.040).\n509\tThe detector achieves higher AUC in the cross-generator setting (0.952 vs.\\ 0.939) because cross-family generation artifacts are more distinctive, and $\\bstar$ adapts seamlessly.\n510\t\n511\t\n512\t\\subsection{Domain-Skewed Contamination}\n513\t\\label{sec:domain}\n514\t\n515\tUnder non-uniform contamination concentrated in code/technical domains (70\\% code), adaptive $\\bstar$ improves PPL by 0.050 (17.730 $\\to$ 17.680).\n516\tPer-batch adaptation is critical: code-heavy batches receive median $\\bstar = 1.35$ while general batches receive $\\bstar = 0.42$, demonstrating automatic domain-aware adjustment.\n517\t\n518\t\n519\t\\subsection{Downstream Evaluation}\n520\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n521\t\n522\tTable~\\ref{tab:downstream} verifies that PPL improvements transfer to downstream tasks on the Llama-3.2-3B model.\n523\t\n524\t\\begin{table}[t]\n525\t\\centering\n526\t\\small\n527\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, $\\rho{=}0.4$, seed42).}\n528\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n529\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n530\t\\toprule\n531\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Uniform} & \\textbf{Adaptive} & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n532\t\\midrule\n533\tMMLU accuracy (\\%)     & 31.8 & 32.5 & $+$0.7 \\\\\n534\tHellaSwag accuracy (\\%) & 52.1 & 53.2 & $+$1.1 \\\\\n535\tSelf-BLEU (4-gram) $\\downarrow$ & 0.42 & 0.38 & $-$0.04 \\\\\n536\t\\bottomrule\n537\t\\end{tabular}\n538\t\\end{table}\n539\t\n540\tMMLU ($+0.7\\%$) and HellaSwag ($+1.1\\%$) improvements are consistent with cleaner pretraining encoding more accurate factual and linguistic knowledge.\n541\tReduced Self-BLEU (0.42 $\\to$ 0.38) indicates that adaptive reweighting mitigates the repetitive output patterns characteristic of synthetic data contamination.\n542\t\n543\t\n544\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n545\t\\section{Analysis}\n546\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n547\t\n548\t\\paragraph{Why minimum intervention works.}\n549\tThe $b$--PPL curve has an asymmetric shape: slow improvement from $b{=}0$ to the plateau ($b \\in [0.3, 0.5]$), then rapid degradation beyond $b{=}1$.\n550\tOver-intervention (ESS-only, $\\nmin{=}1$) pushes $b$ into the steep degradation region, where gradient variance from low ESS overwhelms the benefit of contamination removal.\n551\tMinimum intervention stays on the plateau, preserving effective samples while achieving sufficient decontamination.\n552\t\n553\t\\paragraph{Constraint binding analysis.}\n554\tAt batch size 4: 49.9\\% of batches are clean ($\\bstar = 0$), 20.2\\% are $\\aeff$-bound, and 30.0\\% are ESS-bound.\n555\tIn $\\aeff$-bound batches, $\\bstar = 1.67$ versus $\\bESS = 4.15$ ($2.5\\times$ less intervention), with ESS = 2.60 (30\\% headroom above $\\nmin = 2$).\n556\tAt batch size 16, $\\aeff$ binding increases to 35.2\\% while ESS binding drops to 14.9\\%, confirming $\\aeff$ as the primary constraint at practical scales.\n557\t\n558\t\\paragraph{$\\bstar$ tracks the optimum.}\n559\tWhen contamination increases ($\\rho: 0.4 \\to 0.6$), $\\bstar$ median shifts from 0.87 to 1.45, automatically tracking the new optimal region without re-tuning.\n560\tWhen detector quality improves (TF-IDF $\\to$ ModernBERT), $\\bstar$ median decreases from 0.87 to 0.45, appropriately reducing intervention when scores are more informative.\n561\t\n562\t\n563\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n564\t\\section{Conclusion}\n565\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n566\t\n567\tWe presented dual-constrained adaptive $\\bstar$, a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent in contamination-aware pretraining.\n568\tThe method embodies a minimum intervention principle---apply only as much downweighting as needed---operationalized through an $\\aeff$ quality floor and an ESS stability ceiling.\n569\tAdaptive $\\bstar$ matches grid-search-optimal performance at zero validation cost, is insensitive to its hyperparameters ($\\tau$, $\\nmin$), and scales favorably with contamination severity and model size.\n570\tThe $\\aeff$ constraint is load-bearing: removing it causes the method to degenerate into over-aggressive cleaning with near-uniform performance.\n571\t\n572\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n573\tExperiments use controlled contamination injection rather than naturally contaminated web data; real contamination patterns may differ.\n574\tThe largest model tested is 3B parameters; scaling behavior at 7B+ remains to be validated.\n575\tOur detector evaluation is limited to two detector types; the interaction between detector calibration quality and adaptive $\\bstar$ behavior deserves deeper investigation.\n576\t\n577\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n578\t\n579\t\n580\t\\end{document}\n581\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Po6GWztU94UYxmsY1CE6NF","session_id":"4e76514d-f28c-4641-af3a-08051cc17412","uuid":"c61ebbd3-d064-403e-9a04-0daacb2f7d8b","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:15:00.971Z"}
