{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ea7GJsjofPReXULp6Ncn7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260515T102146\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 4 - Weak Accept (borderline)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-15T11:07:09.904829+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t\n9\t# Dream Review Report: Dual-Constrained Adaptive Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n10\t\n11\t**dream_id:** 20260515T102146 | **review_mode:** full | **reviewers:** R1 (Methods+Experiments), R2 (Novelty+Related Work)\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 1. Paper Summary\n16\t\n17\tThe paper proposes *dual-constrained adaptive b**, a method for selecting the reweighting exponent b in the contamination-aware pretraining formula w=(1−q)^b. Instead of Drayson et al.'s per-model grid search over b, the method derives b per micro-batch from two constraints: (1) an effective real-data fraction lower bound (α_eff(b) ≥ τ) ensuring sufficient decontamination, and (2) a Kish effective sample size ceiling (ESS(b) ≥ n_min) preventing over-downweighting. The solution b* = inf{b ≥ 0 : α_eff(b) ≥ τ} s.t. ESS(b) ≥ n_min embodies a \"minimum intervention principle.\" Experiments on Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B (200M tokens, 40–60% contamination) show adaptive b* matches grid-best within seed variance, with effect amplification at higher contamination (2.5×) and larger model scale (4×).\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## 2. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n22\t\n23\tRelative to the full experiment design (24 imagined experiments), the paper covers approximately 20–21:\n24\t\n25\t| Experiment Category | Covered? | Notes |\n26\t|---|---|---|\n27\t| Grid-b extended (b<0.5, 3 seeds + eval) | ✓ | Table 2, 4 data points |\n28\t| ESS-only ablation | ✓ | Table 3, seed42 only |\n29\t| τ sweep (6 values) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n30\t| Golden Ratio oracle | ✓ | §5.5 |\n31\t| n_min sensitivity | Partial | Table 5 — **n_min=3 omitted** |\n32\t| Batch size ablation (8, 16) | ✓ | Table 6 |\n33\t| ρ=0.6 scaling (datagen + uniform + adaptive + grid) | ✓ | Table 7 |\n34\t| Llama-3.2-3B (setup + uniform + adaptive) | ✓ | Table 8 |\n35\t| Baselines (detect-remove, random) | ✓ | Table 9 |\n36\t| Detector quality (ModernBERT) | ✓ | Table 10 |\n37\t| Cross-generator | ✓ | §5.11 (text only, no table) |\n38\t| Domain-skewed | ✓ | §5.12 (text only, no table) |\n39\t| Downstream eval (MMLU, HellaSwag, Self-BLEU) | ✓ | Table 11 |\n40\t\n41\t**Key gaps:** n_min=3 omitted from Table 5; cross-generator and domain-skewed lack dedicated tables; amplification claims (3B, ρ=0.6) are single-seed.\n42\t\n43\t---\n44\t\n45\t## 3. Reviewer R1 — Methods and Experiments\n46\t\n47\t### Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5\n48\t\n49\t### Strengths\n50\t- **S1:** The minimum intervention formulation is mathematically clean. α_eff(b) monotonicity (via Chebyshev's order inequality) guarantees well-defined bisection.\n51\t- **S2:** The constraint binding analysis is informative: 40% of reweighted batches are α_eff-bound (b*=1.67 vs ESS-only b*=4.15, a 2.5× reduction in intervention).\n52\t- **S3:** Cross-seed stability of b* distribution is high (CV < 2.5%), confirming b* is a data property.\n53\t- **S4:** Experimental controls are careful: identical hyperparameters, consistent holdout evaluation, 3 seeds for primary experiments.\n54\t\n55\t### Weaknesses\n56\t- **W1 (Major): Small effect sizes.** Absolute improvement is 0.040 PPL (0.23% relative) at 0.6B. The adaptive-vs-grid-best gap is ~0.005 PPL — within measurement precision. The 4× amplification at 3B (Δ=0.160) and 2.5× at ρ=0.6 (Δ=0.100) are single-seed claims without multi-seed confirmation.\n57\t- **W2 (Major): n_min knife-edge at batch_size=4.** Table 5 shows only n_min=2 works (n_min=1→17.700, n_min=4→17.690=uniform). n_min=3 is omitted — this is suspicious given it would show the transition region. The method operates at its constraint boundary in 75%+ of batches.\n58\t- **W3 (Major): τ insensitivity paradox.** The τ sweep (Table 4, PPL range=0.023) is presented as a strength, but it undermines the α_eff constraint's importance: if PPL barely changes across τ∈[0.5,0.95], why not just use b=0.5 with no adaptive machinery?\n59\t- **W4 (Moderate): \"Zero tuning cost\" is overstated.** The method replaces one hyperparameter (b) with two (τ, n_min). If b has a broad plateau at [0.3,0.5] (Table 2), a practitioner could set b=0.5 universally with comparable results.\n60\t- **W5 (Moderate): ESS-only ablation is single-seed.** Table 3's critical result (17.685 vs 17.650) needs multi-seed validation. The Δ=0.035 exceeds estimated seed variance (σ≈0.009), but 3-seed confirmation would be much stronger.\n61\t- **W6 (Minor): Algorithm 1 underspecification.** Grid resolution (1000 points), b_max=20.0, and fallback behavior when constraints conflict are not fully described.\n62\t\n63\t### Questions\n64\t1. In only 20.2% of all micro-batches is α_eff binding. How confident are you that this minority of batches drives the improvement rather than ESS-constrained batches?\n65\t2. How should n_min scale with batch size? Is n_min=2 always appropriate?\n66\t3. The adaptive b* median (0.88) is ~2× the grid-best (b=0.5). Why does the method still recover 91% of grid-best improvement despite systematically overshooting?\n67\t4. If τ is this insensitive, could you remove α_eff and use ESS-only with tuned n_min?\n68\t\n69\t---\n70\t\n71\t## 4. Reviewer R2 — Novelty and Related Work\n72\t\n73\t### Score: 4 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5\n74\t\n75\t### Strengths\n76\t- **S1:** The minimum intervention principle (inf vs max) is a genuine conceptual contribution. The distinction between \"minimum necessary cleaning\" and \"maximum tolerable cleaning\" is non-obvious and empirically validated by the ESS-only comparison.\n77\t- **S2:** Transparent experimental methodology with honest limitations disclosure.\n78\t- **S3:** TF-IDF detector sufficiency (77% of ModernBERT value at ~100× lower cost) is a practically valuable finding.\n79\t- **S4:** Clean delineation from Drayson et al. — the paper does not overclaim the weighting formula as novel.\n80\t- **S5:** The α_eff monotonicity proof is correct and ensures bisection well-definedness.\n81\t\n82\t### Weaknesses\n83\t- **W1 (Major): Narrow novelty.** Both α_eff (weighted mean) and ESS (Kish's) are well-established. Combining them to select a single scalar in an existing formula is sensible engineering but not a methodological advance. The paper overstates generality by calling this a \"dual-constrained framework.\"\n84\t- **W2 (Major): \"Dataset-agnostic defaults\" unsupported.** Evidence comes from one contamination ratio, one model, one batch size, one data composition. n_min has a documented failure mode (n_min=3 at bs=4).\n85\t- **W3 (Major): Conflation of \"adaptive\" with \"automatic.\"** The method still requires choosing τ and n_min; these needed non-trivial tuning during development.\n86\t- **W4 (Moderate): Hard threshold q≤0.5 in α_eff is poorly motivated.** The indicator at 0.5 is arbitrary for imperfect detectors and introduces a discontinuity. The paper should justify this choice.\n87\t- **W5 (Moderate): Important related work missing.**\n88\t  - Vehtari et al. (JMLR 2024) on PSIS and ESS diagnostics — directly relevant, uncited\n89\t  - Propensity score methods / inverse probability weighting (Rosenbaum & Rubin, 1983) — structural parallel to α_eff\n90\t  - Off-policy RL importance weighting — ESS-constrained IS is standard practice\n91\t  - Self-paced / curriculum learning — adaptive per-sample weighting during training\n92\t  - AIOLI (Chen et al., ICLR 2025) — adaptive online data mixing, closest \"no grid search\" comparator\n93\t- **W6 (Minor): Abstract claim \"matches grid-search-optimal within seed variance\" is imprecise.** Aggregate recovery is 88.6–91%. Seeds 123 (90.4%) and 456 (80.0%) do not literally match \"within seed variance\" in the statistical sense.\n94\t\n95\t### Questions\n96\t1. Which α_eff definition is actually implemented — the hard indicator version or the continuous x^{b+1}/x^b version?\n97\t2. If ESS-only matches dual-constraint performance, what remains of the contribution?\n98\t3. Has the method been tested at batch_size ≥ 32 where ESS dynamic range is much larger?\n99\t4. Why does b* median (0.88) work well despite being ~2× grid-best (0.5)?\n100\t5. The convergence theory motivation for τ (Wang et al.) requires known contamination proportion. How does this provide guidance when ρ is unknown?\n101\t\n102\t---\n103\t\n104\t## 5. Roundtable Synthesis\n105\t\n106\t### Areas of Agreement\n107\t1. **Effect sizes are marginal at 0.6B** — both reviewers flag 0.04 PPL (0.23%) as borderline for practical significance.\n108\t2. **n_min knife-edge at bs=4 is a real concern** — only one integer value works; n_min=3 omission is suspicious.\n109\t3. **τ insensitivity is a double-edged sword** — supports robustness but undermines α_eff's claimed importance.\n110\t4. **\"Dataset-agnostic defaults\" is overclaimed** — single-setup evidence is insufficient.\n111\t5. **Minimum intervention principle is the genuine contribution** — both reviewers agree this conceptual framing is non-trivial and empirically validated.\n112\t6. **Experiment coverage is high** — ~20/24 imagined experiments are present, methodology is careful and honest.\n113\t\n114\t### Areas of Disagreement\n115\t- **R1 (3) vs R2 (4):** R1 weighs narrow operating regime and marginal effect sizes more heavily; R2 gives more credit to the conceptual contribution and practical utility.\n116\t- **R2 identifies substantial related work gaps** (PSIS, causal inference, off-policy RL) not raised by R1.\n117\t- **R2 flags the α_eff definition inconsistency** (hard indicator vs continuous) as a clarity issue.\n118\t\n119\t### Converged Assessment\n120\t\n121\tThe paper addresses a timely and important problem (synthetic contamination of pretraining data). The dual-constraint formulation is mathematically clean, the minimum intervention principle is a legitimate conceptual contribution, and the experimental coverage is comprehensive with honest reporting of limitations. The TF-IDF sufficiency finding adds practical value.\n122\t\n123\tHowever, the paper has compounding weaknesses that place it at the borderline:\n124\t- Effect sizes are small enough to question practical significance at the tested scale\n125\t- The key amplification claims (3B, ρ=0.6) lack multi-seed confirmation\n126\t- The method introduces two hyperparameters to replace one, with n_min having a narrow working range at small batch sizes\n127\t- τ insensitivity paradoxically weakens the case for α_eff\n128\t- Important related work in importance sampling diagnostics is missing\n129\t- The \"dataset-agnostic\" framing overreaches the evidence\n130\t\n131\t---\n132\t\n133\t## 6. Overall Score\n134\t\n135\t### Consensus: 4 — Weak Accept (borderline)\n136\t\n137\t| Criterion | Score | Notes |\n138\t|---|---|---|\n139\t| Novelty | 3.5 | Modest — applying known IS diagnostics to a specific b-selection problem |\n140\t| Soundness | 4.5 | Formulation is correct; experiments are careful |\n141\t| Significance | 3.0 | Small effect sizes at tested scale; amplification claims under-supported |\n142\t| Clarity | 4.0 | Well-written with clean notation; some claim inflation in abstract |\n143\t| Experiment coverage | 4.5 | Comprehensive ablations; most imagined experiments covered |\n144\t| Reproducibility | 4.0 | Algorithm 1 mostly sufficient; some implementation details missing |\n145\t\n146\t**Overall: 4 (Weak Accept) — borderline, conditional on revisions**\n147\t\n148\t### Required Revisions for Acceptance\n149\t1. **Add n_min=3 to Table 5** — the omission undermines credibility\n150\t2. **Multi-seed confirmation for 3B and ρ=0.6 experiments** — single-seed amplification claims are insufficient\n151\t3. **Cite PSIS (Vehtari et al.) and connect to off-policy IS literature** — the ESS diagnostic is not novel and should be properly attributed\n152\t4. **Tone down \"dataset-agnostic defaults\"** to \"insensitive within the tested configuration\"\n153\t5. **Clarify the α_eff definition** — hard indicator vs continuous formulation discrepancy\n154\t6. **Add paired significance test for adaptive vs grid-best** across 3 seeds\n155\t7. **Add confidence intervals for downstream metrics** (Table 11)\n156\t8. **Tabulate cross-generator and domain-skewed results** instead of text-only mention\n157\t\n158\t### Recommended Revisions\n159\t- Add batch_size=32/64 experiments to demonstrate n_min=2 stability at practical scales\n160\t- Provide wall-clock timing measurements for the bisection overhead\n161\t- Discuss the gradient variance implications of minimum vs maximum intervention formally\n162\t- Correct abstract claim from \"within seed variance\" to \"approaching grid-search-optimal\"\n163\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01W9ZUppDSzV44rVjzo6CkqD","session_id":"4e76514d-f28c-4641-af3a-08051cc17412","uuid":"2402f6ed-b674-45f4-9904-f9ede4bf8de0","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:15:15.813Z"}
